  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cloudy cool Sunday with morning rain and \ndrizzle along and east of Blue Ridge.\n\nCold front which moved through the region Saturday is still\nproviding for abundant cloud cover and cool temperatures. \nA wave of low pressure is rippling northeast along the front\nresulting in rain and drizzle this morning for areas primarily \nalong and east of the Blue Ridge. Models have this precip ending\nby noon with clouds lingering into tonight and then clearing \nMonday. \n\nWith the cool moist airmass in place do not expect much of a\ntemperature rise today. May squeak out a 5 to 10 degree rise if\nrain exits the area as forecast...highs no warmer than the 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions will return by Monday and last \nthroughout the week. Patchy frost may occur early Tuesday and \nWednesday. Temperatures warm for the second half of the week.\n\nHigh pressure will be centered over the Great Lakes Monday then\npass overhead Tuesday. Wind flow should become northwesterly\nMonday scouring the cool wedge east of the Blue Ridge. As the \nweather turns drier, dewpoints should drop into the 40s. \nLow temperatures for Tuesday morning and Wednesday morning may\nsupport some patchy frost in the mountain valleys. Locations \nsuch as the Greenbrier River Valley and Burkes Garden may see \nlows in the mid to upper 30s. The models maintain high pressure\nover the region for the remainder of the week. As the airmass\nmodifies temperatures should moderate for the second half of the\nweek.\n\nNext opportunity for rain is Sunday and Monday Oct 11-12.\nNHC is currently monitoring a broad area of low pressure in the\nSouthwest Gulf of America with increasing confidence this\nfeature may develop into a cyclone in the next 7 days. The\noperational run of the GFS brings this feature north but \nconfidence very low as to whether we will partake in any \ntropical moisture this far north latitude.",
